    "markdown": "# Knowledge Base: cantor-diagonalization-oq-03-oq-01-oq-02\n\n## Problem Understanding\n\nThis problem asks for Rice's theorem as a Lawvere fixed-point theorem instance. The intended interpretation is Val = computable functions and f = complement of a non-trivial semantic property, yielding undecidability of {e | P(phi_e)}.\n\n## Known Inputs\n\n- Parent gallery proof: cantor-diagonalization-oq-03-oq-01 proves the Lawvere fixed-point theorem.\n- Mathlib has partial computability theory support through Mathlib.Computability.\n- The relevant surrounding library area also includes Mathlib.CategoryTheory.\n\n## Current Obstacles\n\n- Need a Lean encoding of computable functions as the evaluation category.\n- Need the point-surjectivity condition for the computable-function case.\n- Need to formalize non-trivial semantic properties in Lean 4.\n\n## Candidate Approaches\n\n1. Directly instantiate the abstract Lawvere fixed-point theorem with computable functions.\n2. Prove Rice's theorem by a classical computability argument, then connect it back to the Lawvere framework.\n"
  },
